{:verdict :pass
 :sounds-like-game "Mostly yes: Hal reads as a companion who shoos you out to real life ('not on a screen', 'Time, by your own clock'), but several keys slip into robotic HUD-label phrasing ('Items: {count}') that is less wry and less Matrix/TRON than the GDD's Hal."
 :findings [{:severity :minor :where ":blueprint-ready (all four lines)" :issue "Every line is the same 'label: {count}' pattern reworded ('Blueprints waiting: {count}.', 'Waiting on your approval: {count}.'). None uses what makes approvals distinct: the grouped tray, the one-line summaries, the before/after view, or the fact that reads never ask." :gdd "§2.1 Approve" :fix "Vary the substance, for example: 'Last night's tray is on the shelf: {count} to look over.', 'Every one has a before and after. Take a look.', 'Say yes and they go real. Say no and they vanish.' Use the count once, and only where a singular count won't read badly."}
            {:severity :minor :where ":morning-brief (all four lines)" :issue "The same count-label line four times ('Items: {count}.', 'Count: {count}.'). It sounds like a log line, not Hal walking to the board at 8:15." :gdd "§3.1 morning brief; §1 pillar 3" :fix "Use different beats: 'Pinned the morning card. {count} things on it, nothing on fire.', 'Card's on the board. Want the short version?', 'Brief's up. Start wherever you like.'"}
            {:severity :minor :where ":last-call" :issue "None of the lines mentions night-shift orders. Overnight work has to be ordered by closing, so last call is the one moment that reminder matters. Picking which work gets the few frontier desks is 'the day's real choice'." :gdd "§2.3 step 4" :fix "Replace one or two variants, for example: 'Last call. Anything for the night crew? Order it before {bedtime}.', 'Fifteen to {bedtime}. Which jobs get the frontier desks tonight?'"}
            {:severity :minor :where ":closing-time" :issue "Only 'Time, by your own clock: {bedtime}.' says that bedtime is the player's own rule. The others ('{bedtime}. Goodnight.') could read as the software closing on its own." :gdd "§2.3 step 5 ('you asked me to send you home')" :fix "Add the GDD's framing: 'You asked me to send you home at {bedtime}. So: home.', 'That's {bedtime}, your rule. The night crew's got it from here. Goodnight.'"}
            {:severity :minor :where ":stay-late \"Okay. The estate stays up with you.\"" :issue "'Stays up' can be read as the estate staying lit or day-mode. Per the GDD, if you stay, the estate stays at night." :gdd "§2.3 step 5" :fix "'Okay. The estate stays at night, and I'll stay with you.'"}
            {:severity :minor :where ":memorial-moved" :issue "'Moved {name} to memorial.' and '{name}'s moved to memorial now.' read like system logs at a moment of grief. '{name} rests in memorial.' and 'I've set {name} a place' are better. No line reassures the player that the reminders stop, even though memorial creatures show no needs." :gdd "§3 twin rule (memorial creatures show no needs)" :fix "Swap the two flat lines for warm ones, for example: 'No more reminders for {name}. Just a quiet spot by the water.', '{name} has a place here now. Nothing to tend, just remember.'"}
            {:severity :minor :where ":coach-habit" :issue "The template '{goal} for {habit}' doubles up when the goal already names the habit ('You said three workouts a week for workout.'). 'You told me {goal} on {habit}' is awkward. 'How's it going?' is generic and drops the GDD's small concrete ask ('One today?')." :gdd "§3.2 ('You said three workouts a week. One today?')" :fix "Lead with {goal} alone and end on one small ask: 'You said {goal}. One today?', 'Your words: {goal}. Want to fit one in?' Keep {habit} for lines where the goal doesn't name it."}
            {:severity :minor :where ":weekly-card" :issue "All four lines say '{hours}, not a score' in different words. None names a trend direction or the paired measure (agents needing you less and you living more), so the variety is thin." :gdd "§1 Win/loss (long-term win)" :fix "Keep one 'not a score' line. Write others about the trend itself, for example: 'Fewer hours in than last week, and the chores still got done in person. That's the good direction.' If the trend direction varies, ask for a {trend} slot."}
            {:severity :minor :where ":agent-stalled \"{agent}'s stalled. I've freed up the desk.\" / \"Freed the desk.\"" :issue "The bridge marks a worker stalled and frees its desk on its own. Hal taking credit blurs who does what. The lines also skip the useful facts that its keycards came back and that the plan refills." :gdd "§3.1 (bridge marks stalled; keycards come back); §2.1 Check the juice" :fix "'{agent} ran out of juice and went quiet. Desk's free, keycards are back.', 'Lost {agent}'s signal. It can pick up again when the plan refills.'"}
            {:severity :minor :where ":program-needed \"I don't have {program} yet for this task.\"" :issue "This states the problem but doesn't ask. The GDD has the twin ask you to load the program right then." :gdd "§2.6 loadout; §2.7 ('I need a program for this one.')" :fix "'Can't do this one without {program}. Load it?'"}
            {:severity :minor :where ":program-loaded" :issue "Accurate, but it misses the 'eyes snap open' load beat that the GDD calls the Matrix moment. All four lines say the same thing: program loaded, building rises." :gdd "§2.6; §2.7 step 2" :fix "Make one variant about the new tool on Hal's belt, for example: 'Felt that? {program}'s on my belt now, and the {building}'s yours.' Keep it original, not the film's line."}
            {:severity :minor :where ":jack-in \"You're jacked in. Let's get going.\" / \"Good, you're here.\"" :issue "Generic greetings. None points to what changed overnight or to what is waiting, which is what jack-in shows." :gdd "§2.3 step 1" :fix "'Welcome back. The night crew left you a few things.', 'You're in. Here's what moved while you slept.'"}
            {:severity :minor :where ":jack-out-household \"They said \\\"{text}\\\". Go, I've got this.\"" :issue "Household text usually ends in punctuation ('Dinner!'), so this renders as '\"Dinner!\".' with doubled punctuation." :gdd "§2.3 step 3" :fix "Drop the trailing period after the quote: '\"{text}\" Go on, I've got this.'"}
            {:severity :minor :where ":chore-done \"Logged it: {name}, {chore}, done.\"" :issue "This reads as a database receipt, not a companion." :gdd "§3 twin rule; §2.6 street cred" :fix "'{chore} for {name}, done. The estate noticed.'"}]}